<h2>The procedural setting</h2>
<p>The appellant faced SC/ST P.S. Case No. 07 of 2026 at Godda, charging BNS offences along with Sections 3(1)(r) and 3(1)(s) of the SC/ST Act — the provisions on intentional insult and abuse by caste name within public view. The Additional Sessions Judge-I, Godda refused anticipatory bail on 23 September 2026. By the time the statutory appeal under Section 14-A(2) reached the High Court, the investigation was complete, the final report filed, and cognizance taken.</p>

<h2>The chargesheet-stage principle</h2>
<p>That completed investigation changed the question. Anticipatory bail guards against arrest during investigation, when custodial interrogation is the State&rsquo;s stated need. Once the chargesheet is in and cognizance taken, that need has evaporated. Relying on <strong>Musheer Alam v. State of U.P.</strong> (2025), the Court applied the Supreme Court&rsquo;s direction: <em>&ldquo;once the investigation is over and charge-sheet is filed then the accused should be asked to appear before the Court concerned&rdquo;</em> — and at that stage, <em>&ldquo;now there is no point in making a formal arrest.&rdquo;</em> The appeal was disposed of with liberty to the appellant to participate in the trial, appearing before the trial court and furnishing bail to its satisfaction.</p>

<h2>Why this route matters under the SC/ST Act</h2>
<p>The Act&rsquo;s Section 18 bar makes anticipatory bail contentious in every atrocities case: the threshold question — whether a prima facie case under the Act is made out — often consumes the entire hearing. The chargesheet-stage principle offers a lawful bypass in the appropriate subset of cases: where investigation has concluded, the accused need not fight the Section 18 battle at all, because the remedy is appearance and regular bail before the Special Court, not pre-arrest protection. The victim-notice requirement, which the Act attaches to bail proceedings, was held dispensable here precisely because nothing on the merits was being decided.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>FIR and investigation</strong> — the Section 18 bar dominates; anticipatory bail requires showing no prima facie case under the Act.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Chargesheet filed, cognizance taken</strong> — custodial interrogation is no longer in play; the Musheer Alam principle applies.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appearance before the Special Court</strong> — the accused appears, furnishes bail to the court&rsquo;s satisfaction, and the trial proceeds.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Statutory appeal if bail is refused</strong> — Section 14-A(2) carries bail orders of the Special Court to the High Court.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">The order decides procedure, not the allegations, which remain for trial. Practitioners should note the forum discipline the Act imposes: challenges to Special Court bail orders travel by Section 14-A(2) appeal, and the timing of the chargesheet can transform the available remedy.</div>
